      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cima%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Herbert Graham Gibson opened the first store in 1900, and a post office followed in 1905, marking Cima’s early commercial beginnings.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cima%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Los Angeles and Salt Lake Railroad climbed 2,000 feet in 20 miles through Cima, making it a vital siding on the steepest grade between Los Angeles and Las Vegas.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23,
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cima%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Today, the Cima Grade remains the steepest section of the Union Pacific line, still hauling freight across the Mojave Desert.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cima%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Cima Dome, a 5,745‑foot volcanic landmark, dominates the skyline and hosts one of California’s densest Joshua tree forests.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cima%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In the early 1900s, Cima served as a watering hole and supply hub for ranchers and miners traversing the Mojave Desert.",
